{
  "rule_id": "R72_46",
  "target": "ts",
  "tier": "T3",
  "governed_construct": "A named function whose return type (declared or inferred by the type checker) is boolean (including a type predicate `x is T`): function declarations, class/object-literal methods, getters, and arrow functions or function expressions bound to a named variable or class property. Anonymous inline callbacks are not instances.",
  "interpretation": "Each governed function must have a name starting with a boolean prefix (is, has, can, should, will, was, were, did, does, are, needs, must, allows, supports, contains, includes) followed by an uppercase letter, e.g. isEmpty, hasRole, canEdit. A boolean-returning named function without such a prefix (validEmail, active, checkPermission, catGuard) is VIOLATED. Whether a function returns boolean is decided by its resolved return type, not by a literal `: boolean` annotation: an unannotated function returning a comparison or a call like RegExp.test, or one annotated with a local alias such as `type Toggle = boolean`, is governed. Functions returning Promise<boolean>, boolean | undefined, or non-boolean types are not governed. A file with no boolean-returning named function is NOT_APPLICABLE. Names in comments and strings are irrelevant.",
